<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Keeps the LastSeek semantic index current.

  Zero LLM, no network, no writes to LastDB. One bounded `lastseek drain`
  (+ `compact` only when something was applied) every 5 minutes, logging what
  it left behind to ~/.lastseek/drain.log.

  Why a timer at all: the index answers whether or not it is fresh, so a
  stopped drain does not fail — it quietly serves a corpus that no longer
  exists. Nothing else surfaces that.

  Why bounded: an unbounded drain over a large backlog wrote 549.80 GB in
  twenty minutes on 2026-08-07 and took the machine down.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.edgevector.lastseek-drain</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>-lc</string>
    <string>exec "$HOME/.lastseek/bin/lastseek-drain-tick"</string>
  </array>

  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>300</integer>

  <!-- The tick logs its own outcome; launchd's stdio only needs to catch a
       failure to start at all. -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/tomtang/.lastseek/launchd.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/tomtang/.lastseek/launchd.log</string>

  <!--
    NOT `Background`, and this is measured rather than preferred.

    `ProcessType: Background` + `Nice` confines the job to efficiency cores on
    Apple Silicon. A/B on an identical 100-file slice of the live inbox, same
    binary, minutes apart on 2026-08-17:

        default QoS      7.4 s wall   611% CPU    74 ms/file
        taskpolicy -b  132.0 s wall   135% CPU  1.32 s /file

    17.8x on wall time and 3.8x on CPU-seconds. Contention was ruled out: a
    foreground probe took 6.3 cores while the throttled live drain was running
    and that drain held 0.37 — it was fenced, not starved.

    The fence spent ~65x of headroom to hold a ~780 files/h workload
    permanently at its edge. On 2026-08-17 arrivals crossed it: ticks/hour fell
    from 8-11 to 1, the `--max-files` cap bound for the first time in eight
    days, and `brain ask` — which is tier-0 LastSeek — was served from a corpus
    30-59 minutes stale while every tick reported `remaining=0`.

    `LowPriorityIO` is KEPT. The measurement above cannot separate its cost
    from the QoS clamp's, and disk politeness is the half that protects other
    work on a shared machine. If lag persists after this change, isolating
    `LowPriorityIO` is the next measurement — not a guess.

    What protects the machine is the BOUND, not the fence: `--max-files` plus
    the tick's own lock. The 2026-08-07 incident that took the host down was an
    UNBOUNDED drain, and it would have taken the host down just as thoroughly
    on efficiency cores.
  -->
  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Standard</string>
  <key>LowPriorityIO</key>
  <true/>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
